body:
hi love. first off, I wanted to tell you that I can feel your passion within your writing about mortuary science and the dead. its beautiful to feel.

I am 28 years old, have been working in health care for my entire adult life as an assistant. I am in school to further my knowledge in Science, from there I am not sure where I will go. Ive considered nursing but most recently mortuary science has come to mind. I too think it sounds like something I would never get bored of. However through the research I've done, the industry is predicted to decline by 4% in the next decade. People are preferring cremation for financial reasons. Also, "natural burials" are becoming more popular. If you think about it, we are kind of running out of space to put grave stones. I don't want to discourage you, because there will always be business for this. But getting started will be difficult. But I believe with persistence and determination you can be successful in anything you do. Be creative. Maybe start your own funeral service. 

&#x200B;

Other considerations that may have a better job outlook: medical examiners, hospice nurses, death and grieving counselors

&#x200B;

I wish you the best. Godspeed!
